{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Playtesting.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Playtesting.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"playtesting playtesting","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Playtesting.md","fileName":"Playtesting.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Playtesting","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 14:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Game Design Methods]] [[Game Production Planning and Milestones]] [[Agile Engineering and Quality]] [[Iterative Game Design Cycle]] [[Game Playtesting Practice]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Design Methods","Game Production Planning and Milestones","Agile Engineering and Quality","Iterative Game Design Cycle","Game Playtesting Practice","Definition of Done","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf","agilegamedevelopment2e.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Playtesting.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Playtesting.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"playtesting playtesting","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Playtesting.md","fileName":"Playtesting.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Playtesting","heading":"Playtesting","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Playtesting observes other people interacting with a game to evaluate the experience rather than to find code defects. It reveals whether players form workable mental models, understand their goals, make meaningful decisions, and remain engaged.\n\nTests should begin early, use clear questions and appropriate participants, and avoid coaching or defending the design. Observations, concise surveys, and player comments identify problems; the designer analyzes recurring evidence and decides how to solve them.\n\nPlaytesting and iteration also reveal whether a game's expressive conversation produces the intended response. That response need not be conventional fun; the test can examine whether the designed system communicates a serious, difficult, or reflective experience clearly.\n\nAgile teams involve players throughout development rather than waiting for a separate final phase. Informal sessions and more controlled studies reinforce the [[Definition of Done]], uncover usability and challenge problems, and give the Product Backlog evidence; they should not be expected to invent the game's breakthrough concept for the team.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Design Methods","Game Production Planning and Milestones","Agile Engineering and Quality","Iterative Game Design Cycle","Game Playtesting Practice","Definition of Done","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf","agilegamedevelopment2e.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Playtesting.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Playtesting.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"playtesting playtesting","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Playtesting.md","fileName":"Playtesting.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Playtesting","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[advancedgamedesign.pdf]]\n[[agamedesignvocabulary.pdf]]\n[[agilegamedevelopment2e.pdf]]\n[[gamesdesignandplay.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Design Methods","Game Production Planning and Milestones","Agile Engineering and Quality","Iterative Game Design Cycle","Game Playtesting Practice","Definition of Done","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf","agilegamedevelopment2e.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}